 <div class=3D"ly"><h2 align=3D"center"><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/=
web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/">Read how to use this Interact=
ive Course</a></h2><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p></di=
v>

<div class=3D"lm"><p>Interactive Batch Course with built-in Reference</p></=
div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>The versatile MS-DOS/MSDOS <span class=3D"bg">Batch La=
nguage</span> automates routine tasks with <span class=3D"bg">Batch files</=
span> (lists of Batch commands). Learn how to write Batch files with this <=
span class=3D"bg">free</span>, interactive Course and built-in Batch Refere=
nce. You need no prior knowledge of Batch files: you're guided through each=
 step on screen in your Web Browser =96 no need for printouts.</p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"><p>What's new or quick if you're in a rush?</p></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>Try our <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/2008081=
5090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/icswref.htm">Batch&nbsp;Reference</a>=
, our <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.alle=
nware.com/mcsw/bus.htm">Batch&nbsp;Troubleshooting</a> tips, or <a href=3D"=
https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/mcsw/in=
dex.htm">Batch&nbsp;Library</a> with example Batch files. If you enjoy our =
interactive tutorials, take our shorter <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/=
web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/sdem/sdmidx.htm">HTML&nbsp;for&=
nbsp;newcomers</a>, or try a brief interactive session that shows our train=
ing ideas in action with our <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/2008081=
5090922/http://www.allenware.com/sdem/qlook.htm">Quick&nbsp;Look</a>. </p><=
/div>
<div class=3D"lm"><p>The full Batch Course or just a <nobr></nobr></p></div=
>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>In our full Course of 21 interactive Lessons, you buil=
d a menu-driven Batch file project for backup, and learn a wide range of Ba=
tch file skills. Try the full Batch File Course, or a <nobr><img src=3D"htt=
ps://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922im_/http://www.allenware.com/cf/bcav=
.gif">click</nobr> for details. Use a link below: the Lesson loads in your =
Browser.</p></div>
<div class=3D"ce"><h4>Lesson Index and Course Syllabus</h4></div>
<a name=3D"Lesson0"></a>
<div class=3D"lm"><p> <img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/2008081509092=
2im_/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/bca.gif"> <a href=3D"https://web.archive=
.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/icsw000.htm">Introduc=
tion</a><br>Get introduced first</p></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>This fully-interactive Course probably isn't like anyt=
hing else you've met on the Web. That's why there's an interactive <a href=
=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/ics=
w/icsw000.htm">Introduction</a>. Think of it as Lesson Zero. You will:
</p><ul><li>Learn to use the interactive Exercises on which the entire Cour=
se is based</li>
<li>Create the working folder used for all the Batch file work that follows=
</li>
<li>Create and configure the <span class=3D"tsk">&nbsp;BatchWindow&nbsp;</s=
pan> (a DVM, or DOS box) for Batch work</li>
<li>Configure and practise with the multiple window layout used in the Less=
ons</li>
<li>Learn to use our clickable, on-screen <span class=3D"bg">Course Referen=
ce</span> for Batch file terms</li></ul><p></p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>The Lesson pages are designed so that you can <nobr><i=
mg src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922im_/http://www.allenwar=
e.com/cf/bcav.gif">(click</nobr> for details) .</p>
<p>The Course is written for Windows 95/98/ME. <nobr></nobr> users who want=
 to try the Course need <nobr><a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/200808=
15090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/nt2000xp.zip">Add-on Pack2.1</a></no=
br> <nobr>(click<img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922im_/h=
ttp://www.allenware.com/icsw/bca.gif"></nobr> for a summary).</p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p><span class=3D"fg">&nbsp;Note&nbsp;</span> Our clickab=
le on-screen Course Reference loads with every Lesson and grows in detail a=
s you progress. We don't load it with this Lesson Index and Syllabus page, =
which gives only an overview. So Batch terms/commands on this Index page ar=
en't clickable.</p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p><span class=3D"fr">&nbsp;Backup&nbsp;</span> <b>Batch =
files are powerful tools. Always backup. Read our <a href=3D"https://web.ar=
chive.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/rcfp.htm#Disclai=
mer">Disclaimer</a>.</b></p></div>
<div class=3D"dvs"><p></p><hr><p></p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"><p>Two ways to study:<br><b>1</b> Graduated Lessons</p></=
div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>In the <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815=
090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/icsw000.htm">Introduction</a> you lear=
nt to create a custom MS-DOS window that is ideal for Batch file work. To f=
ollow an interactive, thorough, and <span class=3D"bg">carefully graduated<=
/span> set of Lessons in which you learn to write your own Batch files, con=
tinue with the <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922/http:/=
/www.allenware.com/icsw/icswidx.htm#Lesson1">Lesson&nbsp;1&nbsp;Syllabus</a=
> now.</p></div>
<div class=3D"lm"><p><b>2</b> Work from reference</p></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>Alternatively, if you're already very familiar with th=
e MS-DOS Batch Language, and need only a DOS command reference with syntax =
examples, see our <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/20080815090922/htt=
p://www.allenware.com/icsw/icswref.htm">Batch&nbsp;Reference</a>:</p></div>
<a name=3D"Lesson21"></a>
<div class=3D"lm"><p> <img src=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/2008081509092=
2im_/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/bca.gif"> <a href=3D"https://web.archive=
.org/web/20080815090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/icswref.htm">Batch&nb=
sp;Reference</a></p></div>
<div class=3D"bd"><p>Contents of <a href=3D"https://web.archive.org/web/200=
80815090922/http://www.allenware.com/icsw/icswref.htm">Batch&nbsp;Reference=
</a> =96 <span class=3D"bg">Batch File Command Reference</span>
</p><ul><li>Batch file commands alphabetically, with cross-references and e=
xamples of use</li>
<li>Batch file commands grouped by <span class=3D"bg">Function</span> (cros=
s-referenced, and with examples)</li>
<li>How to find the <span class=3D"bg">short-form</span> <span class=3D"bb"=
>8.3</span> path to long-name files or folders</li>
<li>How to find UNC <span class=3D"bg">network paths</span> to use Batch fi=
le commands across networks</li>
